{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794558828957,"sessionID":"ses_e2bf59bc7ffeU5sbEdqcp5ihjg","part":{"partID":"prt_1d40aa011001KMxAV9o5ZyxKRD","sessionID":"ses_e2bf59bc7ffeU5sbEdqcp5ihjg","messageID":"msg_1d40a8f61001LbRQPLWKhNvV2T","type":"tool","id":"call_e5db9243a36940f980d810b1","tool":"skill","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"id":"sk-doc"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"sk-doc\">\n# Skill: sk-doc\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-doc, documentation, markdown, authoring, parent-hub, mode-registry, hub-router, workflowmode, packetkind, sk-create-skill, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, sk-create-quality-control, human-voice-rules, hvr, shared-backbone, create-quality-control-pipeline -->\n\n# Documentation Authoring Hub (sk-doc)\n\nOne advisor identity, fifteen workflow modes across fourteen packets, one shared sk-create-quality-control backbone.\n`sk-doc` is the parent hub for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring.\nIt holds NO per-packet logic: it routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each packet keeps its own contract in its nested folder.\nThe cross-cutting sk-create-quality-control pipeline (validators, global standards, frontmatter/llms/template assets) lives once in `shared/` and is consumed by every packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`; `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value, so every hub-root JSON routing file states the same release; `ROUTER.md` keeps its own document version.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring, and for document-quality work. The hub classifies the request, resolves a `workflowMode`, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet | Command |\n|------|------------|--------|---------|\n| **sk-create-skill** | Scaffold an OpenCode skill (and, via `sk-create-skill-parent`, a parent hub with nested mode packets) | `sk-create-skill/` | `/create:skill`, `/create:skill-parent` |\n| **sk-create-readme** | Author a folder README, general or code-folder | `sk-create-readme/` | `/create:readme` |\n| **sk-create-agent** | Scaffold an OpenCode agent (permission/authority frontmatter) | `sk-create-agent/` | `/create:agent` |\n| **sk-create-command** | Scaffold an OpenCode slash command (argument-hint + allowed-tools + router/presentation split) | `sk-create-command/` | `/create:command` |\n| **sk-create-feature-catalog** | Author a feature-catalog inventory package | `sk-create-feature-catalog/` | `/create:feature-catalog` |\n| **sk-create-manual-testing-playbook** | Author a manual-testing-playbook package | `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/` | `/create:manual-testing-playbook` |\n| **sk-create-benchmark** | Author MCP-promotion and behavior packages or inputs | `sk-create-benchmark/` | `/create:benchmark` |\n| **sk-create-changelog** | Author a global component changelog entry (version bump + topology-aware placement). Packet-local changelogs are deprecated | `sk-create-changelog/` | `/create:changelog` |\n| **sk-create-diff** | Produce a local, Git-free before/after review of an edited document (text/Markdown/HTML/DOCX/text-PDF) as a self-contained HTML report | `sk-create-diff/` | `/create:diff` |\n| **sk-create-repo-rule** | Create, revise or retire a repo-local rule under `repo-rules/` and wire it into `REPO RULES.md` (four decision tests refuse most requests) | `sk-create-repo-rule/` | `/create:repo-rule` |\n| **sk-create-with-human-voice** | Apply the Human Voice Rules to prose, or score prose against them, with a scope gate and a re-scan | `sk-create-with-human-voice/` | `/create:with-human-voice` |\n| **sk-create-frontmatter** | Own the YAML frontmatter contract: per-class field rules, the description budget, and the 4-part `version` derivation | `sk-create-frontmatter/` | — (routes via aliases) |\n| **sk-create-goal** | Author or revise a packet `goal.md`: the durable directive, three to seven self-contained completion criteria, and the phase-parent binding table (session-goal setting stays with the goal hooks) | `sk-create-goal/` | `/create:goal` |\n| **sk-create-quality-control** | Validate / score / optimize an EXISTING document (extract → DQI → HVR → validate) | `sk-create-quality-control/` | (routes via aliases) |\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Code implementation, tests, or debugging — use `sk-code`.\n- Git worktree, branch, commit, PR, or release work — use `sk-git`.\n- Spec-folder workflow / memory / save context — use `system-spec-kit`.\n- A measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source) — use `sk-design-md-generator`.\n- The `shared/` backbone is consumed by the packets, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) selects a mode: `hub-router.json` `vocabularyClasses` is the one authored routing vocabulary.\nThe `mode-registry.json` aliases, each packet's `Keyword triggers:` line and the root `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against it.\n`sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-hub-vocabulary-source.cjs` fails on the `alias`, `stage-two`, `triggers` and `packets` legs; its `stage-two-reverse` leg only reports.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) lives in the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md), which maps the request's authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources that mode loads.\nThe two layers stay separate: the hub never emits leaf paths, and the surface router never re-decides the mode.\nThe advisor routes every documentation and authoring query to the single identity `sk-doc`.\nResolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \"<task>\"`.\nAct on the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default.\nAn unclear documentation intent asks for disambiguation, because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public packet key a request resolves to, such as `sk-create-skill`.\n- **`packetKind`**: `workflow` for every sk-doc packet, because this hub has no surface axis.\n- **`backendKind`**: `template-scaffold` for the create-* generators and `sk-create-quality-control` for the quality-control mode.\n- **packet**: a nested `sk-create-*` folder that keeps its own authoring contract, references, assets and scripts.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each packet's discriminator, aliases, command and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's signals, vocabulary classes, default fallback resource and bundle rules.\n- **`vocabularyClasses`**: the one authored routing vocabulary that the registry aliases, the `Keyword triggers:` lines and the `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, which map an authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources its mode loads.\n- **leaf**: a canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **`defer`**: the outcome that asks for disambiguation, which this hub reaches because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n- **shared backbone**: the `shared/` copy of the sk-create-quality-control pipeline that every packet consumes.\n- **`graph-metadata.json`**: the one advisor identity for the whole skill, which no packet or `shared/` directory carries.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-doc/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-packet logic)\n  ROUTER.md              # stage-two surface router (authoring intent -> leaf sets)\n  mode-registry.json     # the fifteen-mode discriminator + advisorRouting (single source of truth)\n  hub-router.json        # router signals + vocabulary classes\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  changelog/  manual-testing-playbook/  benchmark/  feature-catalog/\n  sk-create-skill/  sk-create-readme/  sk-create-agent/  sk-create-command/\n  sk-create-feature-catalog/  sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/\n  sk-create-benchmark/  sk-create-changelog/  sk-create-diff/\n  sk-create-repo-rule/  sk-create-frontmatter/  sk-create-goal/\n  sk-create-with-human-voice/  sk-create-quality-control/    # nested workflow packets\n  scripts/               # facade symlinks -> shared/ + owning packets (tool paths only)\n  shared/                # sk-create-quality-control backbone: validators, global standards, shared assets\n```\n\nEach packet is self-contained (its own `SKILL.md`, `README.md`, `changelog/`, and moved `references/`/`assets/`/`scripts/`) and carries **no** `graph-metadata.json`, so the advisor discovers exactly one `sk-doc` identity.\n\n### Shared backbone\n`shared/` holds the universal sk-create-quality-control pipeline consumed by every packet:\n- Generic validator scripts in `shared/scripts/`.\n- Cross-cutting standards and vocabulary in `shared/references/`.\n- Shared templates in `shared/assets/`.\n\nThe generic validator `shared/scripts/cite-drift-scan.mjs` counts and resolves the `<path>.<ext>:<line>` citations in every tracked skill doc.\nIt makes zero model calls by default.\n`--jev` runs the backend and needs `--out <dir>`, so every call is recorded, and the Jev arm and the advisory live in the sibling `shared/scripts/classifier-cite-drift-scan.mjs`.\n`validate_document.py` runs the same check as a non-blocking advisory on the document it validates.\nIt prints `cite-drift advisory:` lines only when a Jev credential is stored, never changes the exit code, and `JEV_FEATURE_CITE_DRIFT=0` (older name: `SKDOC_CITE_DRIFT_CHECK=0`) skips it.\nThe `sk-doc/scripts/` root directory keeps facade symlinks pointing inward to `shared/` and the owning packets so tool paths resolve.\nThere are no hub-root `assets/` or `references/` aggregation directories: consumers reference each packet's own `assets/`/`references/` or the `shared/` backbone directly.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a packet through `mode-registry.json`; never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep authoring contracts in the packets; the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one identity.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the root `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` in sync with `leaf-manifest.json` and `leaf-aliases.json` — the leaf sets dual-read to canonical typed pairs at the one contract boundary.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the sk-create-quality-control pipeline as one shared source under `shared/`, consumed by the packets.\n- **ALWAYS** keep changelogs as real files at the hub and in each packet — never symlinked.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-packet authoring logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** add a surface axis or a `surfaceBundle` outcome — sk-doc is workflow-only.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new documentation workflow is needed — extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet; do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- A packet would become a near-empty shell (generic doctrine, no type-specific behavior) — fold it into `shared/` instead.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (fifteen modes across fourteen packets, `packetKind: workflow`).\n- Hub router: `hub-router.json` (signals + vocabulary classes).\n- Surface router: `ROUTER.md` (authoring intent to packet-local leaf sets).\n- Advisor descriptor: `description.json`; skill-graph identity: `graph-metadata.json`.\n- Packets: `sk-create-skill/`, `sk-create-readme/`, `sk-create-agent/`, `sk-create-command/`, `sk-create-feature-catalog/`, `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/`, `sk-create-benchmark/`, `sk-create-changelog/`, `sk-create-diff/`, `sk-create-repo-rule/`, `sk-create-frontmatter/`, `sk-create-goal/`, `sk-create-with-human-voice/`, `sk-create-quality-control/`.\n- Shared backbone: `shared/scripts/`, `shared/references/`, `shared/assets/`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/source.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","title":"skill","metadata":{"metadata":{"name":"sk-doc","directory":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc","truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill_content name=\"sk-doc\">\n# Skill: sk-doc\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-doc, documentation, markdown, authoring, parent-hub, mode-registry, hub-router, workflowmode, packetkind, sk-create-skill, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, sk-create-quality-control, human-voice-rules, hvr, shared-backbone, create-quality-control-pipeline -->\n\n# Documentation Authoring Hub (sk-doc)\n\nOne advisor identity, fifteen workflow modes across fourteen packets, one shared sk-create-quality-control backbone.\n`sk-doc` is the parent hub for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring.\nIt holds NO per-packet logic: it routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each packet keeps its own contract in its nested folder.\nThe cross-cutting sk-create-quality-control pipeline (validators, global standards, frontmatter/llms/template assets) lives once in `shared/` and is consumed by every packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`; `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value, so every hub-root JSON routing file states the same release; `ROUTER.md` keeps its own document version.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring, and for document-quality work. The hub classifies the request, resolves a `workflowMode`, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet | Command |\n|------|------------|--------|---------|\n| **sk-create-skill** | Scaffold an OpenCode skill (and, via `sk-create-skill-parent`, a parent hub with nested mode packets) | `sk-create-skill/` | `/create:skill`, `/create:skill-parent` |\n| **sk-create-readme** | Author a folder README, general or code-folder | `sk-create-readme/` | `/create:readme` |\n| **sk-create-agent** | Scaffold an OpenCode agent (permission/authority frontmatter) | `sk-create-agent/` | `/create:agent` |\n| **sk-create-command** | Scaffold an OpenCode slash command (argument-hint + allowed-tools + router/presentation split) | `sk-create-command/` | `/create:command` |\n| **sk-create-feature-catalog** | Author a feature-catalog inventory package | `sk-create-feature-catalog/` | `/create:feature-catalog` |\n| **sk-create-manual-testing-playbook** | Author a manual-testing-playbook package | `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/` | `/create:manual-testing-playbook` |\n| **sk-create-benchmark** | Author MCP-promotion and behavior packages or inputs | `sk-create-benchmark/` | `/create:benchmark` |\n| **sk-create-changelog** | Author a global component changelog entry (version bump + topology-aware placement). Packet-local changelogs are deprecated | `sk-create-changelog/` | `/create:changelog` |\n| **sk-create-diff** | Produce a local, Git-free before/after review of an edited document (text/Markdown/HTML/DOCX/text-PDF) as a self-contained HTML report | `sk-create-diff/` | `/create:diff` |\n| **sk-create-repo-rule** | Create, revise or retire a repo-local rule under `repo-rules/` and wire it into `REPO RULES.md` (four decision tests refuse most requests) | `sk-create-repo-rule/` | `/create:repo-rule` |\n| **sk-create-with-human-voice** | Apply the Human Voice Rules to prose, or score prose against them, with a scope gate and a re-scan | `sk-create-with-human-voice/` | `/create:with-human-voice` |\n| **sk-create-frontmatter** | Own the YAML frontmatter contract: per-class field rules, the description budget, and the 4-part `version` derivation | `sk-create-frontmatter/` | — (routes via aliases) |\n| **sk-create-goal** | Author or revise a packet `goal.md`: the durable directive, three to seven self-contained completion criteria, and the phase-parent binding table (session-goal setting stays with the goal hooks) | `sk-create-goal/` | `/create:goal` |\n| **sk-create-quality-control** | Validate / score / optimize an EXISTING document (extract → DQI → HVR → validate) | `sk-create-quality-control/` | (routes via aliases) |\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Code implementation, tests, or debugging — use `sk-code`.\n- Git worktree, branch, commit, PR, or release work — use `sk-git`.\n- Spec-folder workflow / memory / save context — use `system-spec-kit`.\n- A measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source) — use `sk-design-md-generator`.\n- The `shared/` backbone is consumed by the packets, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) selects a mode: `hub-router.json` `vocabularyClasses` is the one authored routing vocabulary.\nThe `mode-registry.json` aliases, each packet's `Keyword triggers:` line and the root `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against it.\n`sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-hub-vocabulary-source.cjs` fails on the `alias`, `stage-two`, `triggers` and `packets` legs; its `stage-two-reverse` leg only reports.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) lives in the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md), which maps the request's authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources that mode loads.\nThe two layers stay separate: the hub never emits leaf paths, and the surface router never re-decides the mode.\nThe advisor routes every documentation and authoring query to the single identity `sk-doc`.\nResolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \"<task>\"`.\nAct on the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default.\nAn unclear documentation intent asks for disambiguation, because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public packet key a request resolves to, such as `sk-create-skill`.\n- **`packetKind`**: `workflow` for every sk-doc packet, because this hub has no surface axis.\n- **`backendKind`**: `template-scaffold` for the create-* generators and `sk-create-quality-control` for the quality-control mode.\n- **packet**: a nested `sk-create-*` folder that keeps its own authoring contract, references, assets and scripts.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each packet's discriminator, aliases, command and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's signals, vocabulary classes, default fallback resource and bundle rules.\n- **`vocabularyClasses`**: the one authored routing vocabulary that the registry aliases, the `Keyword triggers:` lines and the `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, which map an authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources its mode loads.\n- **leaf**: a canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **`defer`**: the outcome that asks for disambiguation, which this hub reaches because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n- **shared backbone**: the `shared/` copy of the sk-create-quality-control pipeline that every packet consumes.\n- **`graph-metadata.json`**: the one advisor identity for the whole skill, which no packet or `shared/` directory carries.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-doc/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-packet logic)\n  ROUTER.md              # stage-two surface router (authoring intent -> leaf sets)\n  mode-registry.json     # the fifteen-mode discriminator + advisorRouting (single source of truth)\n  hub-router.json        # router signals + vocabulary classes\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  changelog/  manual-testing-playbook/  benchmark/  feature-catalog/\n  sk-create-skill/  sk-create-readme/  sk-create-agent/  sk-create-command/\n  sk-create-feature-catalog/  sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/\n  sk-create-benchmark/  sk-create-changelog/  sk-create-diff/\n  sk-create-repo-rule/  sk-create-frontmatter/  sk-create-goal/\n  sk-create-with-human-voice/  sk-create-quality-control/    # nested workflow packets\n  scripts/               # facade symlinks -> shared/ + owning packets (tool paths only)\n  shared/                # sk-create-quality-control backbone: validators, global standards, shared assets\n```\n\nEach packet is self-contained (its own `SKILL.md`, `README.md`, `changelog/`, and moved `references/`/`assets/`/`scripts/`) and carries **no** `graph-metadata.json`, so the advisor discovers exactly one `sk-doc` identity.\n\n### Shared backbone\n`shared/` holds the universal sk-create-quality-control pipeline consumed by every packet:\n- Generic validator scripts in `shared/scripts/`.\n- Cross-cutting standards and vocabulary in `shared/references/`.\n- Shared templates in `shared/assets/`.\n\nThe generic validator `shared/scripts/cite-drift-scan.mjs` counts and resolves the `<path>.<ext>:<line>` citations in every tracked skill doc.\nIt makes zero model calls by default.\n`--jev` runs the backend and needs `--out <dir>`, so every call is recorded, and the Jev arm and the advisory live in the sibling `shared/scripts/classifier-cite-drift-scan.mjs`.\n`validate_document.py` runs the same check as a non-blocking advisory on the document it validates.\nIt prints `cite-drift advisory:` lines only when a Jev credential is stored, never changes the exit code, and `JEV_FEATURE_CITE_DRIFT=0` (older name: `SKDOC_CITE_DRIFT_CHECK=0`) skips it.\nThe `sk-doc/scripts/` root directory keeps facade symlinks pointing inward to `shared/` and the owning packets so tool paths resolve.\nThere are no hub-root `assets/` or `references/` aggregation directories: consumers reference each packet's own `assets/`/`references/` or the `shared/` backbone directly.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a packet through `mode-registry.json`; never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep authoring contracts in the packets; the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one identity.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the root `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` in sync with `leaf-manifest.json` and `leaf-aliases.json` — the leaf sets dual-read to canonical typed pairs at the one contract boundary.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the sk-create-quality-control pipeline as one shared source under `shared/`, consumed by the packets.\n- **ALWAYS** keep changelogs as real files at the hub and in each packet — never symlinked.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-packet authoring logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** add a surface axis or a `surfaceBundle` outcome — sk-doc is workflow-only.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new documentation workflow is needed — extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet; do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- A packet would become a near-empty shell (generic doctrine, no type-specific behavior) — fold it into `shared/` instead.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (fifteen modes across fourteen packets, `packetKind: workflow`).\n- Hub router: `hub-router.json` (signals + vocabulary classes).\n- Surface router: `ROUTER.md` (authoring intent to packet-local leaf sets).\n- Advisor descriptor: `description.json`; skill-graph identity: `graph-metadata.json`.\n- Packets: `sk-create-skill/`, `sk-create-readme/`, `sk-create-agent/`, `sk-create-command/`, `sk-create-feature-catalog/`, `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/`, `sk-create-benchmark/`, `sk-create-changelog/`, `sk-create-diff/`, `sk-create-repo-rule/`, `sk-create-frontmatter/`, `sk-create-goal/`, `sk-create-with-human-voice/`, `sk-create-quality-control/`.\n- Shared backbone: `shared/scripts/`, `shared/references/`, `shared/assets/`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/source.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"id\":\"sk-doc\"}"},"time":{"start":1794558828561,"end":1794558828957}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794558828962,"sessionID":"ses_e2bf59bc7ffeU5sbEdqcp5ihjg","part":{"partID":"prt_1d40a9eee001TPCfermIP8QG4f","sessionID":"ses_e2bf59bc7ffeU5sbEdqcp5ihjg","messageID":"msg_1d40a8f61001LbRQPLWKhNvV2T","type":"tool","id":"call_0dc6d405b0324bc89093a5d9","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md, lines 1-88\n1: ---\n2: title: \"advisor_recommend Command\"\n3: description: \"Native command that returns prompt-safe skill recommendations with lane attribution, lifecycle metadata, fail-open freshness semantics and an optional compiled-routing enrichment.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"advisor_recommend\"\n6:   - \"mcp recommend tool\"\n7:   - \"native recommend\"\n8:   - \"skill recommendation tool\"\n9:   - \"compiledRoute enrichment\"\n10: version: 0.9.3.0\n11: ---\n12: \n13: # advisor_recommend Command\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: Expose the native scoring pipeline as a command that any runtime can call through the CLI front door, with prompt-safe attribution and lifecycle-aware redirects.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n22: \n23: `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` implements the command. Input is validated by `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` (Zod strict). The public response always includes the resolved `workspaceRoot` plus `effectiveThresholds`, where `effectiveThresholds` publishes the active `confidenceThreshold`, `uncertaintyThreshold` and `confidenceOnly` mode after request overrides are merged with defaults. Output also carries prompt-safe `recommendations[]`, optional `laneBreakdown[]` when `includeAttribution: true`, lifecycle redirect metadata (`redirectFrom`, `redirectTo`, `status`), `freshness`, `trustState`, `generatedAt`, `cache`, optional `warnings` and optional `abstainReasons`. Fail-open states such as disabled or absent freshness still preserve `workspaceRoot` and `effectiveThresholds` in the envelope so callers can inspect the resolved repository scope and active routing thresholds even when no recommendations are returned. The handler enforces sanitization at the envelope boundary (see `lib/derived/sanitizer.ts`) and never echoes prompt text into response metadata. Cache behavior is tied to generation (see [`daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md`](../../feature-catalog/daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md)).\n24: \n25: Shadow comparison is response-visible but durable recording is default-off. The handler calls the shadow-delta sink only when `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_SHADOW_DELTA_PATH` is set or `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_SHADOW_DELTA_ENABLED=1` / `true`; otherwise `advisor_recommend` writes no shadow delta file.\n26: \n27: The caller-supplied `workspaceRoot` is bounded by `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`, which canonicalizes the path and accepts it only when it resolves under the repo root, `os.tmpdir()` or the `/tmp` symlink form, or sits in a project whose `.skilled` (or `.opencode`) links to this source tree, the way `/doctor:symlink` links one, meaning its realpath equals this source tree's own entry. The `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_WORKSPACE_ALLOWLIST` env var extends that set with extra colon-separated prefixes for CI or test runners, each canonicalized before it is trusted. The flag is unset by default, so the bounding logic stays repo-and-tmpdir-only unless an operator opts in.\n28: \n29: ### Compiled-Routing Enrichment (`compiledRoute`)\n30: \n31: Each recommendation carries an optional `compiledRoute` field (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:308`, `z.record(z.string(), z.unknown()).optional()`). `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`'s `enrichCompiledRoutes()` attaches it only when at least one recommended `skillId` is both in the fixed 7-hub `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` set and currently permitted by the tri-state `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING` flag (`compiledRoutingEnabledForHub()`, from `../lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts`). Otherwise the schema re-parse is skipped entirely and the output returns unchanged. When enrichment runs, `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub <skillId> --prompt-stdin`, writes the prompt to its stdin and attaches the parsed result. The one exception is a result whose `servingAuthority` is `\"legacy\"`: the field then stays absent, because a legacy sentinel is never surfaced as if it were a compiled decision. A subprocess failure is handled the same way: silently no `compiledRoute` field, with an optional debug-gated stderr breadcrumb via `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING_DEBUG`, so a probe failure degrades to exactly what an ineligible or flag-off recommendation already looks like. In short, `compiledRoute` is attached only for an eligible, flag-permitted hub that is genuinely serving compiled. It is intentionally absent for every other combination: flag off, hub not in `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS`, legacy sentinel or probe failure. See [`feature-flag-governance.md`](../../../system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/governance/feature-flag-governance.md) for the flag's phased-default and serving-status contract, and each eligible hub's own `compiled-routing-and-legacy-fallback.md` catalog leaf for the hub-side directive this enrichment mirrors. The front door runs from the request's `workspaceRoot`, and a caller may name another checkout there, such as a worktree under the repository root. So the handler first checks that the front door it would run reads `--prompt-stdin`. An ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n32: \n33: When a recommendation gets a `compiledRoute`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` also asks `attachCommand()` in `lib/command-attach.ts` for a slash command and sets the optional `command` field when one comes back (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:310`, `z.string().min(1).optional()`). `attachCommand()` names a command only for a `route` decision with `selectionKind: 'single'` and exactly one target, and only when the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table maps that hub and `workflowMode` to a command. A bundle, a multi-target route or a mode with no command leaves `command` off. For `system-deep-loop` the prompt must also contain `/deep:` or a phrase from `DEPTH_WORDING`, so a prompt that only names a topic such as research or review gets no deep-loop command.\n34: \n35: The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. When a caller passes `false` the handler skips `enrichCompiledRoutes()` on both the fresh and the cached return (`handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:563-565`, `632-634`), so no `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess starts and no recommendation carries `compiledRoute` or `command`. The prompt hooks pass `true` (`hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:238`), because the `command` field is derived from the compiled route. The OpenCode plugin does not pass the option and keeps receiving `compiledRoute`. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:287`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:23`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:57`).\n36: \n37: The request option `options.compiledRouteTopOnly` is an optional boolean that defaults to false. When a caller passes `true`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` runs the route lookup for the first recommendation only and returns every later one unchanged, so only the top pick can carry `compiledRoute` or `command`. Each lookup is a `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess, so this saves one subprocess for each further eligible hub. The prompt hooks pass `true` with `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the brief leads with the top pick. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:290`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:24`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:58`).\n38: \n39: A hub also leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row. `enrichCompiledRoutes()` takes the `command` fields present in the result as the commands already offered, so one command never takes two slots.\n40: \n41: ### Recommendation Contract (`kind`, `command`, `policy`, `ownerSkillId`)\n42: \n43: Each recommendation carries up to four optional fields that let a reader act on it without a second lookup. `kind` is `'skill'` or `'command'` and says which face the pick is, so a caller can tell a skill entry from a command row that scored as a candidate. `command` is the runnable slash string, such as `/speckit:save`. `policy` is `'run'`, `'start'` or `'ask'` and says how a caller may run that command. `ownerSkillId` names the skill that owns the command, which is the grouping key when several commands belong to one hub. All four stay optional in `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` so a response from a runtime that predates them still parses.\n44: \n45: `lib/command-attach.ts` is the single source for the values. Its generated `COMMAND_ROWS` block maps each id a scorer can return, whether the TypeScript id, the Python id or the entry id, to `{ command, policy?, ownerSkillId }`, and `commandRowFor(id)` reads it with an own-key check so an inherited name such as `constructor` never resolves to a row. `publicRecommendation()` in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` looks one up for the recommendation's own id and copies its `command`, its `policy` when present and its `ownerSkillId`, and it forwards the scorer's `kind`. An id with no row adds none of the three. A command attached through the compiled route takes its `policy` from `COMMAND_POLICIES[command]` because that generated table is keyed by runnable string, and it takes no `ownerSkillId` because the recommendation already names the hub.\n46: \n47: The renderer and the degraded path read the same contract. `lib/render.ts` builds the brief label `<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)` from the recommendation's own fields first and falls back to the generated row for anything omitted, so a hook that predates the fields still prints a runnable command. The CLI degraded path in `skill-advisor-cli.ts` emits a `kind: 'command'` entry with all four fields when the prompt contains the row's bare slash command, and otherwise emits the row's `ownerSkillId` as a `kind: 'skill'` entry so the fallback brief names a real skill instead of a raw row id.\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 3. SOURCE FILES\n52: \n53: ### Implementation\n54: \n55: | File | Layer | Role |\n56: |---|---|---|\n57: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` | Handler | Source reference |\n58: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` | Schema | Source reference |\n59: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tools/` | Command surface | Source reference |\n60: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts` | Shared | `attachCommand()`, the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table and the `DEPTH_WORDING` gate that name the slash command for a single routed mode. |\n61: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts` | Shared | `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` eligibility set, `DEFAULT_ON_HUBS` cohort, and the tri-state flag parser the enrichment gate reads. |\n62: | `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs` | Script | Promoted CLI front door `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to. |\n63: \n64: ### Validation And Tests\n65: \n66: | File | Type | Role |\n67: |---|---|---|\n68: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Validation reference |\n69: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` | Automated test | `includeCompiledRoute: false` skips enrichment on fresh and cached returns. `compiledRouteTopOnly: true` looks up the first recommendation alone. A command that ranks through its own row is not offered again by its hub. |\n70: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/legacy/advisor-privacy.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Validation reference |\n71: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/compiled-routing-consumption.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Bridge child-env allowlist forwarding, `buildNativeBrief` decision threading, and `=0` cache-invalidation coverage for the `compiledRoute` enrichment. |\n72: | `.skilled/bin/compiled-routing-flag-propagation.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Tri-state flag propagation coverage. |\n73: | `Playbook scenarios [NC-001](../../manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/native-recommend-happy-path.md), [NC-004](../../manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/ambiguous-brief-rendering.md), [NC-005](../../manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/lifecycle-redirect-metadata.md).` | Manual playbook | Source reference |\n74: \n75: ---\n76: \n77: ## 4. SOURCE METADATA\n78: \n79: - Group: Command surface\n80: - Canonical catalog source: `feature-catalog.md`\n81: - Feature file path: `cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md`\n82: \n83: Related references:\n84: \n85: - [02-advisor-status.md](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-status.md).\n86: - [03-advisor-validate.md](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-validate.md).\n87: - [04-compat-entrypoint.md](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/compat-entrypoint.md).\n88: - [feature-flag-governance.md](../../../system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/governance/feature-flag-governance.md) — the `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING` flag-governance entry this enrichment's gate reads.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md, lines 1-88\n1: ---\n2: title: \"advisor_recommend Command\"\n3: description: \"Native command that returns prompt-safe skill recommendations with lane attribution, lifecycle metadata, fail-open freshness semantics and an optional compiled-routing enrichment.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"advisor_recommend\"\n6:   - \"mcp recommend tool\"\n7:   - \"native recommend\"\n8:   - \"skill recommendation tool\"\n9:   - \"compiledRoute enrichment\"\n10: version: 0.9.3.0\n11: ---\n12: \n13: # advisor_recommend Command\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: Expose the native scoring pipeline as a command that any runtime can call through the CLI front door, with prompt-safe attribution and lifecycle-aware redirects.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n22: \n23: `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` implements the command. Input is validated by `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` (Zod strict). The public response always includes the resolved `workspaceRoot` plus `effectiveThresholds`, where `effectiveThresholds` publishes the active `confidenceThreshold`, `uncertaintyThreshold` and `confidenceOnly` mode after request overrides are merged with defaults. Output also carries prompt-safe `recommendations[]`, optional `laneBreakdown[]` when `includeAttribution: true`, lifecycle redirect metadata (`redirectFrom`, `redirectTo`, `status`), `freshness`, `trustState`, `generatedAt`, `cache`, optional `warnings` and optional `abstainReasons`. Fail-open states such as disabled or absent freshness still preserve `workspaceRoot` and `effectiveThresholds` in the envelope so callers can inspect the resolved repository scope and active routing thresholds even when no recommendations are returned. The handler enforces sanitization at the envelope boundary (see `lib/derived/sanitizer.ts`) and never echoes prompt text into response metadata. Cache behavior is tied to generation (see [`daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md`](../../feature-catalog/daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md)).\n24: \n25: Shadow comparison is response-visible but durable recording is default-off. The handler calls the shadow-delta sink only when `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_SHADOW_DELTA_PATH` is set or `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_SHADOW_DELTA_ENABLED=1` / `true`; otherwise `advisor_recommend` writes no shadow delta file.\n26: \n27: The caller-supplied `workspaceRoot` is bounded by `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`, which canonicalizes the path and accepts it only when it resolves under the repo root, `os.tmpdir()` or the `/tmp` symlink form, or sits in a project whose `.skilled` (or `.opencode`) links to this source tree, the way `/doctor:symlink` links one, meaning its realpath equals this source tree's own entry. The `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_WORKSPACE_ALLOWLIST` env var extends that set with extra colon-separated prefixes for CI or test runners, each canonicalized before it is trusted. The flag is unset by default, so the bounding logic stays repo-and-tmpdir-only unless an operator opts in.\n28: \n29: ### Compiled-Routing Enrichment (`compiledRoute`)\n30: \n31: Each recommendation carries an optional `compiledRoute` field (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:308`, `z.record(z.string(), z.unknown()).optional()`). `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`'s `enrichCompiledRoutes()` attaches it only when at least one recommended `skillId` is both in the fixed 7-hub `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` set and currently permitted by the tri-state `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING` flag (`compiledRoutingEnabledForHub()`, from `../lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts`). Otherwise the schema re-parse is skipped entirely and the output returns unchanged. When enrichment runs, `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub <skillId> --prompt-stdin`, writes the prompt to its stdin and attaches the parsed result. The one exception is a result whose `servingAuthority` is `\"legacy\"`: the field then stays absent, because a legacy sentinel is never surfaced as if it were a compiled decision. A subprocess failure is handled the same way: silently no `compiledRoute` field, with an optional debug-gated stderr breadcrumb via `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING_DEBUG`, so a probe failure degrades to exactly what an ineligible or flag-off recommendation already looks like. In short, `compiledRoute` is attached only for an eligible, flag-permitted hub that is genuinely serving compiled. It is intentionally absent for every other combination: flag off, hub not in `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS`, legacy sentinel or probe failure. See [`feature-flag-governance.md`](../../../system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/governance/feature-flag-governance.md) for the flag's phased-default and serving-status contract, and each eligible hub's own `compiled-routing-and-legacy-fallback.md` catalog leaf for the hub-side directive this enrichment mirrors. The front door runs from the request's `workspaceRoot`, and a caller may name another checkout there, such as a worktree under the repository root. So the handler first checks that the front door it would run reads `--prompt-stdin`. An ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n32: \n33: When a recommendation gets a `compiledRoute`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` also asks `attachCommand()` in `lib/command-attach.ts` for a slash command and sets the optional `command` field when one comes back (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:310`, `z.string().min(1).optional()`). `attachCommand()` names a command only for a `route` decision with `selectionKind: 'single'` and exactly one target, and only when the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table maps that hub and `workflowMode` to a command. A bundle, a multi-target route or a mode with no command leaves `command` off. For `system-deep-loop` the prompt must also contain `/deep:` or a phrase from `DEPTH_WORDING`, so a prompt that only names a topic such as research or review gets no deep-loop command.\n34: \n35: The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. When a caller passes `false` the handler skips `enrichCompiledRoutes()` on both the fresh and the cached return (`handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:563-565`, `632-634`), so no `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess starts and no recommendation carries `compiledRoute` or `command`. The prompt hooks pass `true` (`hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:238`), because the `command` field is derived from the compiled route. The OpenCode plugin does not pass the option and keeps receiving `compiledRoute`. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:287`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:23`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:57`).\n36: \n37: The request option `options.compiledRouteTopOnly` is an optional boolean that defaults to false. When a caller passes `true`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` runs the route lookup for the first recommendation only and returns every later one unchanged, so only the top pick can carry `compiledRoute` or `command`. Each lookup is a `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess, so this saves one subprocess for each further eligible hub. The prompt hooks pass `true` with `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the brief leads with the top pick. The option is declared in the zod schema (`schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:290`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:24`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:58`).\n38: \n39: A hub also leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row. `enrichCompiledRoutes()` takes the `command` fields present in the result as the commands already offered, so one command never takes two slots.\n40: \n41: ### Recommendation Contract (`kind`, `command`, `policy`, `ownerSkillId`)\n42: \n43: Each recommendation carries up to four optional fields that let a reader act on it without a second lookup. `kind` is `'skill'` or `'command'` and says which face the pick is, so a caller can tell a skill entry from a command row that scored as a candidate. `command` is the runnable slash string, such as `/speckit:save`. `policy` is `'run'`, `'start'` or `'ask'` and says how a caller may run that command. `ownerSkillId` names the skill that owns the command, which is the grouping key when several commands belong to one hub. All four stay optional in `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` so a response from a runtime that predates them still parses.\n44: \n45: `lib/command-attach.ts` is the single source for the values. Its generated `COMMAND_ROWS` block maps each id a scorer can return, whether the TypeScript id, the Python id or the entry id, to `{ command, policy?, ownerSkillId }`, and `commandRowFor(id)` reads it with an own-key check so an inherited name such as `constructor` never resolves to a row. `publicRecommendation()` in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` looks one up for the recommendation's own id and copies its `command`, its `policy` when present and its `ownerSkillId`, and it forwards the scorer's `kind`. An id with no row adds none of the three. A command attached through the compiled route takes its `policy` from `COMMAND_POLICIES[command]` because that generated table is keyed by runnable string, and it takes no `ownerSkillId` because the recommendation already names the hub.\n46: \n47: The renderer and the degraded path read the same contract. `lib/render.ts` builds the brief label `<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)` from the recommendation's own fields first and falls back to the generated row for anything omitted, so a hook that predates the fields still prints a runnable command. The CLI degraded path in `skill-advisor-cli.ts` emits a `kind: 'command'` entry with all four fields when the prompt contains the row's bare slash command, and otherwise emits the row's `ownerSkillId` as a `kind: 'skill'` entry so the fallback brief names a real skill instead of a raw row id.\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 3. SOURCE FILES\n52: \n53: ### Implementation\n54: \n55: | File | Layer | Role |\n56: |---|---|---|\n57: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` | Handler | Source reference |\n58: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` | Schema | Source reference |\n59: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tools/` | Command surface | Source reference |\n60: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts` | Shared | `attachCommand()`, the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table and the `DEPTH_WORDING` gate that name the slash command for a single routed mode. |\n61: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts` | Shared | `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` eligibility set, `DEFAULT_ON_HUBS` cohort, and the tri-state flag parser the enrichment gate reads. |\n62: | `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs` | Script | Promoted CLI front door `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to. |\n63: \n64: ### Validation And Tests\n65: \n66: | File | Type | Role |\n67: |---|---|---|\n68: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Validation reference |\n69: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` | Automated test | `includeCompiledRoute: false` skips enrichment on fresh and cached returns. `compiledRouteTopOnly: true` looks up the first recommendation alone. A command that ranks through its own row is not offered again by its hub. |\n70: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/legacy/advisor-privacy.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Validation reference |\n71: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/compiled-routing-consumption.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Bridge child-env allowlist forwarding, `buildNativeBrief` decision threading, and `=0` cache-invalidation coverage for the `compiledRoute` enrichment. |\n72: | `.skilled/bin/compiled-routing-flag-propagation.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Tri-state flag propagation coverage. |\n73: | `Playbook scenarios [NC-001](../../manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/native-recommend-happy-path.md), [NC-004](../../manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/ambiguous-brief-rendering.md), [NC-005](../../manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/lifecycle-redirect-metadata.md).` | Manual playbook | Source reference |\n74: \n75: ---\n76: \n77: ## 4. SOURCE METADATA\n78: \n79: - Group: Command surface\n80: - Canonical catalog source: `feature-catalog.md`\n81: - Feature file path: `cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md`\n82: \n83: Related references:\n84: \n85: - [02-advisor-status.md](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-status.md).\n86: - [03-advisor-validate.md](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-validate.md).\n87: - [04-compat-entrypoint.md](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/compat-entrypoint.md).\n88: - [feature-flag-governance.md](../../../system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/governance/feature-flag-governance.md) — the `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING` flag-governance entry this enrichment's gate reads."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794558828270,"end":1794558828962}}}}
